{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1082", "sample_idx": 24, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "He woke to the sound of birds chirping in perfect, looping cadence—too rhythmic, too clean—and the sun hung motionless at the exact angle it had been at yesterday, and the day before, and the day before that. His alarm clock blinked 7:03 a.m. with unchanging precision, and when he reached for his coffee mug, it slid across the counter as if on rails, stopping exactly where it always did, three inches from the edge. Outside, his neighbor waved at him from across the street, but the wave was identical to the one from yesterday—same tilt of the head, same half-smile, same duration. He checked his phone: no new messages, no notifications, no time stamps that had changed. The world felt… scripted. He whispered, “Is this real?” and the trees rustled in response with a sound like a loading screen. A faint chime echoed in his skull, and a translucent prompt appeared in the corner of his vision: “Quest: Find the Exit. Progress: 0%.”\n\n***\n\nHe ran through town, past the same smiling shopkeeper who offered him the same pastry with the same phrase—“Fresh out of the oven, sir!”—and the same dog that barked three times before wagging its tail in perfect synchronization. He smashed a window, screamed at the sky, tore his shirt—nothing changed. Then he noticed the faint glow beneath his skin, a grid of glowing lines tracing his veins like a HUD. He pressed his palm to his chest, and a menu flickered into existence: “Player: Elias Voss. Health: 100%. Sanity: 87%. Objective: Accept the Game.” He laughed, then sobbed, then whispered, “I didn’t sign up for this.” The world paused. The birds fell silent. A single line of text appeared before him, glowing gold: “Welcome, Player. You were always here.” And then, for the first time, he remembered: he had chosen this. He had clicked “New Game.”", "logprobs": [{"token": "He", "logprob": -0.6198107600212097}, {"token":